llvm-4.0.0 backport patch: a new-expression's storage is aligned for its type only (PS4)

This patch CHANGES CODE GENERATION for the initialisation a new-expression
emits into the storage of a replaceable global operator new. r283789
(Richard Smith, 2016-10-10, the re-commit of r283722, "P0035R4: Semantic
analysis and code generation for C++17 overaligned allocation", first
release clang 4.0) takes that storage as aligned to min(NewAlign, the
type's size), NewAlign 16 bytes on x86-64 (lib/Basic/TargetInfo.cpp:46),
so a zeroing store of a value-initialised object of 16 bytes or more is
`vmovaps`. Before it the storage was assumed aligned for the allocated
type (and the array cookie). In the game region, 142 call sites of _Znwm
or _Znam store a zeroed vector register at offset 0 of the result within
16 instructions, all unaligned (vmovups/vmovdqu); every one of the 34
zeroed `vmovaps` stores after a call lands in an object whose size is a
multiple of 16 (vector members). With this patch 0x137c3e0 compiles to
the base's bytes.

--- a/tools/clang/lib/CodeGen/CGExprCXX.cpp
+++ b/tools/clang/lib/CodeGen/CGExprCXX.cpp
@@ -1565,18 +1565,11 @@
     RValue RV =
       EmitNewDeleteCall(*this, allocator, allocatorType, allocatorArgs);
 
-    // If this was a call to a global replaceable allocation function that does
-    // not take an alignment argument, the allocator is known to produce
-    // storage that's suitably aligned for any object that fits, up to a known
-    // threshold. Otherwise assume it's suitably aligned for the allocated type.
+    // PS4: assume only that the allocation function returns something
+    // suitably aligned for the element type, plus the cookie if there is one,
+    // as before r283789 (clang 4.0): no alignment from the new-expression's
+    // operator new itself (-fnew-alignment).
     CharUnits allocationAlign = allocAlign;
-    if (!E->passAlignment() &&
-        allocator->isReplaceableGlobalAllocationFunction()) {
-      unsigned AllocatorAlign = llvm::PowerOf2Floor(std::min<uint64_t>(
-          Target.getNewAlign(), getContext().getTypeSize(allocType)));
-      allocationAlign = std::max(
-          allocationAlign, getContext().toCharUnitsFromBits(AllocatorAlign));
-    }
 
     allocation = Address(RV.getScalarVal(), allocationAlign);
   }
